WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.033
K nearest neighbors.

2
00:00:02.333 --> 00:00:07.567
A classifier that never trains, and the ancestor of every vector search you will ever run.

3
00:00:08.767 --> 00:00:11.567
The idea is as simple as asking your neighbors.

4
00:00:11.867 --> 00:00:16.633
To classify a new point, find the k closest training points and let them vote.

5
00:00:16.933 --> 00:00:18.356
There is no training step.

6
00:00:18.356 --> 00:00:20.600
The model is literally the training data.

7
00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:24.000
Picture two classes of points on a plane.

8
00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:26.667
Now a new point arrives, marked in yellow.

9
00:00:26.967 --> 00:00:30.225
Its three nearest neighbors are one blue and two orange.

10
00:00:30.225 --> 00:00:33.367
The majority wins, so the new point is called a flare.

11
00:00:34.567 --> 00:00:36.733
Here is the work behind each prediction.

12
00:00:37.033 --> 00:00:38.467
We take the query point.

13
00:00:38.767 --> 00:00:41.467
Measure the distance to every single training row.

14
00:00:41.767 --> 00:00:43.067
Keep the k closest.

15
00:00:43.367 --> 00:00:44.400
And take a vote.

16
00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:46.833
So what is the catch?

17
00:00:47.133 --> 00:00:51.167
There is no training, but every prediction has to scan the whole dataset.

18
00:00:51.467 --> 00:00:56.133
And in many dimensions, everything is roughly the same distance from everything else.

19
00:00:56.433 --> 00:01:00.133
That is the curse of dimensionality: nearest stops meaning anything.

20
00:01:01.333 --> 00:01:02.533
Here it is in code.

21
00:01:02.833 --> 00:01:05.667
First, the distance from the query to every row.

22
00:01:05.967 --> 00:01:08.933
Then we sort, and keep the indices of the three nearest.

23
00:01:09.233 --> 00:01:12.913
Finally we count the labels and return the most common one.

24
00:01:12.913 --> 00:01:15.033
That matches scikit-learn exactly.

25
00:01:16.233 --> 00:01:17.233
Now the payoff.

26
00:01:17.533 --> 00:01:21.233
When your RAG system gets a question, it turns it into an embedding.

27
00:01:21.533 --> 00:01:25.168
The vector database then fetches the k nearest mission logs.

28
00:01:25.168 --> 00:01:27.833
That is literally a nearest neighbor search.

29
00:01:28.133 --> 00:01:33.467
At scale, exact search gives way to approximate indexes like HNSW.

30
00:01:34.667 --> 00:01:35.555
Let's recap.

31
00:01:35.555 --> 00:01:37.405
KNN has no training step.

32
00:01:37.405 --> 00:01:39.033
The data is the model.

33
00:01:39.333 --> 00:01:42.500
It predicts by a majority vote of the k nearest points.

34
00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:46.200
It is costly at inference, and cursed by high dimensions.

35
00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:49.190
And it is the ancestor of vector search.

36
00:01:49.190 --> 00:01:50.333
Next up: K-Means.
